  playing pawn to e5 and then after pawn  to c5 then pawn a3 this is the main line  for this specific variation saying hey  bishop you need to make a decision right  now decides to go and get that double  pawn  on the C file as we talked about and  then Queen to a5 so attacking this pawn  why this force to defend that doesn't  really want to play Queen to d2 blocking  in his dark square Bishop this can't  really do anything and then Knight to e2  that just seems even worse because it's  blocking your light square bishop you  really don't want to be fiing killing  your bishop to the King side that often  in this opening so Bishop to d2 somewhat  kind of a forced move Queen to a for  deciding to played pretty aggressive  with this Queen moving a lot instead of  developing as other pieces attacking  controlling this d4 square and then  Queen to g4 and you know right away I  thought we just see nice hood C R to f3  makes a lot of sense simple develop move  protects the square but we do see Queen  to g4 which is definitely the better  option it protects the square and also  attack this pawn here on g7 and black  has two options  I usually opt for pawn to g6 this does  defend it but black decided Dana play  that King to FA giving up his ability to  castle and does not want to go and play  this pawn to g6 so with that  Fabiano for him that's kind of the green  light she wants to start attacking he  knows it's going to be very difficult  you know the knight can come here to h6  we can't come to f6 since he's  controlling it and his King it's going  to have be very difficult for him to  have a lot of protection with his King  since you can't castle it's going to be  hard for him to get these other pieces  over to protect that so that's where he  starts to get his other pieces involved  into the game Knight to f3 pawn to b6  and ask you know he could have tried  something else maybe Queen takes on c2  rook here c1 Queen up efore check maybe  if the Queen comes off the board K